[Music]  so  hi i'm shannon bailey the chief curator  at the world chess hall of fame  and here we're looking at our current  exhibition  power and check chess and the american  presidency  it's so fun for us to be able to present  exhibitions  that celebrate our current events and  it was very fitting for us because we  have so many political and presidential  artifacts in our collection  to celebrate the 2020 presidential  election  we have so many different chess sets  it's really great  to be able to pull these out of our  permanent collection and in fact we  actually got great donations for this  just particular  exhibition here we see our presidents  involved in everything  from our our kind of battles with russia  here we have the american revolution  and the civil war because abraham  lincoln  general grant and george washington  were all chess players  we have fun chess sets that look at just  particular races  here we have this very fun cartoony bill  clinton versus bob dole chess set  with really great details including even  bill clinton eating his mcdonald's  we also show a great variety of how  chess sets can be made as well in this  case we've got beautiful carved wood  sets  plastic paper clay and 3d printed  one of our favorite chess sets in this  entire show  is this seemingly simple wooden chest  set here  this set was actually made from a tree  that had to be cut down that was  believed to have been planted  by thomas jefferson at monticello thomas  jefferson  was the most prolific chess playing  president in our entire nation's history  and this tree had to be cut down  and to commemorate its history it was  made into chess sets and some other  beautiful artifacts  we were lucky enough to get the final  chess set for our permanent collection  in this exhibition you get to learn how  every single president of the united  states  was involved with chess some of them  didn't play and some of them were  fantastic chess players  some of them helped promote the game  here we have some really great  artifacts in our collection from gerald  ford and  george bush who we have signed letters  of them  promoting national chess day and  national chess week  this exhibition will be on view through  the inauguration  so if you haven't had a chance to come  down and visit it live  you still have plenty of chance to do  that also you can visit it on our  website  worldchesthof.org

